 distinctive painted patterns and powerful lights, lighthouses represent        
 humanity's determination to conquer the challenges of the sea.                 
                                                                                
 The engineering behind lighthouses is remarkable for its era. Early            
 lighthouses relied on open flames, then candles, and eventually the Fresnel    
 lens—a revolutionary invention that used prisms and refraction to project      
 light across vast distances. The engineering challenges of building these      
 structures on unstable coastal terrain required innovation and courage from    
 the builders and the lighthouse keepers who maintained them in isolation.      
                                                                                
 Today, most lighthouses have been automated, making the role of the lighthouse 
 keeper a largely historical occupation. Yet these structures remain beloved    
 landmarks, attracting tourists and photographers who appreciate their          
 architectural beauty and historical significance. Many lighthouses now serve   
 as museums, bed-and-breakfasts, or art installations, finding new purposes     
 while maintaining their connection to maritime history.                        
                                                                                
